2026-03-31 06:31:18,943 INFO services.websocket_service: 📨 WS MESSAGE RECEIVED client_id=c15578a8-8d71-4cbb-8504-7360892f9066 type=test_tts data={'type': 'test_tts', 'text': 'Streaming protocols further complicate the situation. When audio is transmitted over a real-time channel, such as a WebRTC connection, the data must be packetized and delivered in a strictly ordered sequence. Any mismatch between the production rate of audio data and the consumption rate on the receiving end can lead to buffer underruns or overruns. In practical terms, this means that parts of the audio may be skipped, delayed, or replaced entirely by newer data.\n\nTo ensure reliable performance, it is essential to design the pipeline with clear boundaries between stages. Each component should explicitly define whether it operates on complete audio buffers or continuous streams. Mixing these paradigms without careful handling is a frequent source of bugs. Additionally, implementing proper logging and monitoring can help identify where in the pipeline data loss occurs, allowing for targeted fixes rather than guesswork.\n\nUltimately, building a robust text-to-speech system is less about choosing the most advanced model and more about ensuring that the surrounding infrastructure can handle the data it produces. Stability, consistency, and correct data flow management are what separate a system that works in demos from one that performs reliably in real-world conditions.'}
